<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Generalization</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method is applicable to any Enc-MLP-Dec architectures, while previous methods rely on specific vision encoders. For instance, VisPruner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> necessitates [<span id="A2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CLS</span>] as attention measures, therefore, fails to adapt to the latest QwenVL series.
Although VisionZip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> provides implementation for Qwen2.5VL, it suffers from a significant decline in instruction-following capacity at high pruning rates, as shown in Fig. <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ B.4 Efficiency Analysis ‣ Appendix B More Experiments ‣ MI-Pruner: Crossmodal Mutual Information-guided Token Pruner for Efficient MLLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Due to its limited generalization and lack of theoretical guarantees, the model becomes increasingly prone to hallucinations under aggressive compression, <span id="A2.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, keeping 25% vision tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T10" class="ltx_table">
